{
  "coreName": "Flycast",
  "systemId": "DC",
  "author": "Flyinghead and the Flycast contributors",
  "url": "https://github.com/flyinghead/flycast",
  "romFile": "disc",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    256,
    16,
    64,
    16,
    1536
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A Dreamcast is BIG: 16MB of system RAM, 16MB of video RAM, 8MB for the AICA and 32MB for the Elan, and with no virtual memory in a sandbox those are ordinary allocations inside the guest heap. Sized like a smaller machine, an allocation fails quietly and the machine writes through a null base. The MMAP arena is the one that had to grow for the OpenGL renderer, and not the heap: musl sends every LARGE allocation to mmap, and a whole Mesa is mostly large allocations. With it at 256 MiB OSMesaCreateContext simply returned null, and growing sbrk instead changed nothing.",
  "video": {
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), so a state this core makes opens again in a later session - its greenzone survives a reload instead of being discarded.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the tile clip and the scaler registers a game sets. 640x480 is the most a Dreamcast displays.",
    "width": 640,
    "height": 480,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "samplesPerFrame": 8192,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".gdi": "DC",
    ".cdi": "DC",
    ".chd": "DC",
    ".cue": "DC",
    ".elf": "DC"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "Dreamcast Controller",
    "_comment": "FOUR PORTS, and one superset of controls covering every device any of them may be set to (the port<N> settings). A Dreamcast port takes more than a gamepad, and the declared control list is static - it cannot follow the settings - so every device's controls are declared for every player and a port ignores the ones its device does not have. The controller family (gamepad, arcadeStick, twinStick, xl) all read the SAME state and differ only in which parts of it the device reports: the retail pad has no C, D or Z and no second d-pad, the twin stick has the second d-pad and no analog at all, and the PantherDC 'xl' has everything including a second analog stick. Mouse and lightGun are different wires: the mouse's axes are RELATIVE motion and the gun's are an absolute position on the screen, which is why they are separate axes rather than the stick's.",
